    "narrative": "The first discovery round yields two scientifically important signals, but they are on opposite sides of the registered hypotheses because all displayed estimates are already signed to the claim direction. H1 (TP53 loss, registered negative direction) has signed estimate -0.829 with 95% interval [-0.891, -0.767]; because the claimed direction was negative and the signed estimate/interval are entirely negative, this is strong evidence against the registered negative association and suggests instead a substantial positive raw difference. I therefore reject H1 as registered and will propose a reversed TP53-loss claim. H3 (KEAP1 loss, registered positive direction) has signed estimate +0.810 with interval entirely above the 0.15 threshold, supporting a large positive association in the supplied data. I accept H3 as an association within this dataset and request independent validation because it is a major accepted effect. H4 has a signed estimate +0.139 with positive 95% interval [0.065, 0.213], so it supports the claimed direction but does not clearly exceed the 0.15 threshold; the point estimate is below threshold and the interval includes threshold values, so I keep it unresolved/deferred rather than accept. H2, H5, H6, H7, H8, H9, and H12 have small signed effects whose intervals include zero and/or do not meet the 0.15 threshold, so I reject them as unsupported in the registered direction. H10 and H11 are not valid comparisons because the continuous signature contrast using exact numeric exposed/comparator values produced zero cells; these results provide no evidence about the signature and need re-specification with a proper dichotomized categorical contrast. Multiplicity is modest but not zero; the H1/H3 effects are far larger than the threshold and have narrow intervals, so they are unlikely to be explained by chance alone, though unmeasured confounding and correlated predictors remain possible. Validation is requested for H3 only; this does not establish causality or generalizability beyond this dataset unless the independent validation supports it.",
    "research_notes": "Round 1 evidence: TP53 loss shows large effect opposite to the registered negative claim, so register a reversed TP53 claim. KEAP1 loss is accepted as a large positive association; independent validation requested. CDKN2A loss is directionally supportive but borderline around the 0.15 threshold. STK11, KRAS, MYC, constructed markers, and KRAS x STK11 interaction are not meaningful in the registered directions. Continuous signature contrasts failed due exact-value filtering/coding; do not repeat numeric exposed=0.5/comparator=-0.5 as a valid test. Next steps: test reversed TP53 claim, find a valid representation for high/low signature contrasts or consider condition-based stratification/interaction designs, and monitor technical confounders if effects persist."
}

