 "narrative": "The newly delivered validations sharpen the current evidence. H3 is the main KEAP1-loss association; the response-deadline validation gives a signed estimate +0.788 with stringent validation interval [0.698, 0.879], far above the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed positive direction. Because H3 is positive, the raw interpretation is that KEAP1-loss samples have a higher composite dependency score, i.e. weaker composite dependency, by about 0.79 units. I therefore keep H3 accepted and active as a large dataset-level association. H27 is the Ky-library TP53-loss robustness claim; validation gives +0.866 [0.709, 1.023], again far above threshold and directionally consistent with the earlier Ky discovery estimate +0.787. This confirms that the TP53-loss association is not limited to the dominant Avana library within this dataset, though it remains an association rather than a causal or external claim.\n\nH44, PTEN loss in the stronger-dependency direction, is the important borderline case. The original discovery gave +0.239 [0.145, 0.332], and independent validation gives +0.279 [0.138, 0.419] under a stringent independent interval. Both point estimates exceed the 0.15-unit threshold and agree in direction: raw PTEN loss appears to correspond to a more negative dependency_composite, i.e. stronger composite dependency, by roughly 0.24 to 0.28 units. However, both the discovery lower bound and the validation lower bound are at or just below the prespecified threshold. Given that PTEN was introduced in a broad predictor screen and that the independent interval does not confidently place the entire effect above 0.15 units, I keep H44 unresolved and active rather than accepting it as a scientifically meaningful predictor. It is a promising moderate association, not yet an established meaningful effect.\n\nTaken together, the supported dataset-level claims remain: TP53 loss is associated with weaker dependency (H13 and related robustness claims), KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker dependency (H3, validated), and SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger dependency (H26, validated). The conditional analyses strengthen these by showing persistence when the other dominant losses are excluded: SMARCA4 loss in TP53-/KEAP1-intact samples H33 +0.814, KEAP1 loss in SMARCA4-intact samples H37 +0.809, and TP53 loss in SMARCA4-intact samples H38 +0.828. Library-specific checks also support technical stability: TP53 and KEAP1 associations in Avana and Ky libraries H14/H15 and H27/H28, and SMARCA4 in Avana and Ky libraries H34/H35. These are dataset associations and robustness checks, not claims of causal independence, gene-specific knockout mechanisms, or external generalizability.\n\nNegative or inconclusive results are also informative. ARID1A loss does not generalize the SMARCA4-loss finding in the registered stronger-dependency direction. RB1, SMAD4, and BRCA2 loss are not supported for stronger dependency in this dataset. CDKN2A loss remains consistently near but below threshold, so it should not be accepted as a meaningful predictor without clearer evidence. MTAP, STK11, KRAS, MYC amplification, and the constructed research markers were not meaningful in their tested directions, and screen ROC-AUC did not modify TP53 or KEAP1 effects in a meaningful positive interaction. Some planned heterogeneity questions remain unresolved, especially PTEN robustness/independence and SMARCA4 subtype or stemness specificity.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is to follow up PTEN loss directly: H47 and H48 test whether the borderline PTEN effect is stable in the dominant Avana and smaller Ky libraries, and H49 tests whether it survives in the TP53-, KEAP1-, and SMARCA4-intact population. If H47/H49 preserve estimates above 0.15 units, H44 could be accepted as a fourth moderate predictor association, perhaps with caveats. If PTEN attenuates in triple-intact cells or behaves inconsistently across libraries, H44 should remain unresolved, be narrowed to a stratum, or be reconsidered as a weak/nonspecific finding. Secondary priority is H41/H42, which test whether SMARCA4-loss association is specific to adenocarcinoma or interacts with high stemness. All conclusions remain within this dataset and should not be interpreted as causal dependency or single-gene knockout biology.",
 "research_notes": "Current supported dataset-level associations: H3 KEAP1 loss positive/validated (weaker dependency), H13 TP53 loss positive (weaker dependency), H26 SMARCA4 loss negative/validated (stronger dependency). Robustness/conditional accepted claims include Avana/Ky checks and exclusion of co-loss patterns: TP53/KEAP1 effects persist in SMARCA4-intact, SMARCA4 persists in TP53/KEAP1-intact, SMARCA4 persists in Avana/Ky. H27 Ky TP53 now validated +0.866 [0.709,1.023]. PTEN H44 remains borderline unresolved: discovery +0.239 [0.145,0.332], validation +0.279 [0.138,0.419]. Do not accept PTEN yet because stringent validation lower bound is below 0.15 and PTEN emerged from broad screening. Next priority: run/select H47 (PTEN Avana), H48 (PTEN Ky), and especially H49 (PTEN TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4 triple-intact). If PTEN remains >0.15 and not attenuated, accept with caveats; if attenuated, keep unresolved/reject or narrow. CDKN2A remains near threshold and closed as inconclusive. ARID1A, RB1, SMAD4, BRCA2, MTAP, STK11, KRAS, MYC, markers null/not supported. Keep final claims dataset-level associations only."
