  "narrative": "The accepted predictor set is stable, so the next comparisons should mainly resolve the two open issues: (1) whether CDKN2A loss is a meaningful predictor or only a borderline association, and (2) whether the moderate PTEN stronger-dependency association survives technical-quality, adenocarcinoma, high-mutation-burden, and slow-growth contexts when competing large effects are removed. I therefore register clean quadruple-intact CDKN2A comparisons in low aneuploidy, high screen quality with high Cas9 activity, and high mutation burden. For PTEN, I register clean comparisons in combined high screen quality/high Cas9 activity, clean adenocarcinoma with high Cas9, clean high mutation burden, and clean slow-growth samples. These are direct subgroup mean differences, not formal interaction claims, and they test whether the moderate PTEN effect is robust rather than confined to permissive quality or proliferation states. I also register robustness checks for the large accepted predictors in orthogonal genomic contexts: TP53 and KEAP1 in high mutation burden and low aneuploidy, and SMARCA4 in low aneuploidy. A persistent above-threshold signed effect in these strata would support broad supplied-dataset additivity; attenuation or nullity would motivate a more conditional interpretation. All claims remain dataset associations, not causal knockout effects or externally generalizable biology.",
